    <h1 class="h1" style="max-width:900px">Conversion-focused web design: principles that turn visitors into customers.</h1>
    <div class="bi-meta"><div class="bi-tag"><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 12V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h8l9 9-9 9-9-9z"/><circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.5"/></svg>Web Design</div><div><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="16" rx="2"/><path d="M3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4"/></svg><time datetime="2026-10-31">31 October 2026</time></div><div><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M12 7v5l3 2"/></svg>10 min read</div></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section-tight" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap bi-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-img"><img src="../assets/img/blog/donusum-odakli-web-tasarim.svg" alt=""></div>
      <p class="bi-lead">Hundreds of people visit your site every month, yet the phone doesn’t ring and forms stay empty? The problem is usually not traffic but design. <b>Conversion-focused web design</b> puts what you want visitors to do at the centre and builds every element to serve that action.</p>
      <p>Aesthetics matter, but a website’s job is not to be admired; it is to produce results. A quote request, a purchase, an appointment, a subscription or a phone call: whichever is valuable to you, every design decision should be made with it in mind.</p>
      <p>In this article we explain what conversion is and how it is measured, then the core principles based on user experience (UX) research that we also apply in our own projects. At the end of each principle you’ll find concrete suggestions you can check on your own site right away.</p>
      <h2 id="donusum-nedir" class="bi-h2" data-n="01">What is a conversion and how is the conversion rate calculated?</h2>
      <p>A conversion is a visitor completing an action on your site that is valuable to you. The conversion rate is the number of visitors who complete that action divided by the total number of visitors. A site that gets 2,000 visitors a month and collects 40 quote requests has a conversion rate of 2 percent.</p>
      <p>It is useful to split conversions into two types:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Macro conversion:</b> The main goal. A purchase, a quote form, an appointment, a phone call.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Micro conversion:</b> Steps toward the goal. Downloading a catalogue, signing up for a newsletter, viewing the pricing page, clicking the WhatsApp button.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>There is no single answer to “what is a good conversion rate?”; it varies widely by industry, traffic source, product price and target action. The healthiest comparison is your own site’s figures from last month and last year. Measure first, then improve.</p>
      <h2 id="ilk-ekran" class="bi-h2" data-n="02">The first screen: answer “why you?” in a few seconds</h2>
      <p>The area visitors see without scrolling when they open the page is where they decide whether to stay. This area must clearly answer three questions: <b>What do you do, who do you do it for, and what should the visitor do now?</b></p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A headline that states the benefit:</b> Instead of generic slogans like “Solutions for the future”, a concrete promise like “Track apartment dues from a single panel”.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A supporting subheading:</b> Make the promise concrete in one sentence: for whom, how, and how quickly.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A single primary button:</b> The first step the visitor should take.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Real visuals:</b> Your product’s screen, your team or your work. Stock photos raise suspicion, not trust.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Five-second test:</b> Show your homepage to someone unfamiliar with your site for five seconds, then close it. Ask: “What does this company do, and what does it want from you?” If they can’t answer, it’s time to rewrite your first screen.</div></div>
      <h2 id="cta" class="bi-h2" data-n="03">Call-to-action (CTA) design: one goal, clear copy</h2>
      <p>Call-to-action buttons are the heart of conversion-focused design. Every page should have one main goal, and the visual hierarchy on the page should point to it.</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Copy that starts with a verb and describes the outcome:</b> “Get a free quote” instead of “Submit”, “Book a demo” instead of “Click here”.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Contrast:</b> The primary button should stand out from other elements in colour and size. Secondary actions should look more subdued.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>The right place:</b> Once on the first screen, and again at the end of relevant sections on long pages. On mobile, within thumb reach.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Few options:</b> Five different buttons in the same area keep visitors from deciding. Fewer options make decisions easier.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Adding a short reassuring note right below the button also helps, such as “It’s free, we reply within 24 hours” or “No credit card required”.</p>
      <h2 id="hiyerarsi" class="bi-h2" data-n="04">Visual hierarchy and scannable content</h2>
      <p>People don’t read web pages; they scan them. Nielsen Norman Group’s eye-tracking research shows that users largely follow an “F”-shaped scanning pattern on text-heavy pages, and that this pattern holds on both desktop and mobile. Information outside this pattern is largely missed. In right-to-left languages such as Arabic, the pattern is mirrored.</p>
      <p>Ways to design for this behaviour:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Start each section with a descriptive subheading; someone reading only the headings should get the gist of the page.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Keep paragraphs short, put key information at the start of sentences and bold key phrases.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Use bulleted lists, comparison tables and icon cards instead of long explanations.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Don’t fear white space; a cramped page goes unread no matter how much information it holds.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <blockquote><div class="bi-q"><svg class="ic" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7H5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3M19 7h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3"/></svg></div>If visitors have to think to find what they are looking for, the design isn’t doing its job.</blockquote>
      <h2 id="formlar" class="bi-h2" data-n="05">Form design: every field is an obstacle</h2>
      <p>Forms are where conversions happen, and usually where the most drop-off occurs. Baymard Institute’s e-commerce checkout research shows that an average checkout flow displays 14.88 form fields by default, whereas an ideal flow could be completed with 7–8. The same principle applies to quote and contact forms: every extra question you ask is a reason to abandon the form.</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Ask only what you need:</b> At first contact, a name, a phone number or email and a short message are usually enough. You can get the details in conversation.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Put labels above fields:</b> Don’t rely only on faint placeholder text inside the box; it disappears once you start typing.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Open the right keyboard:</b> A numeric keypad for phone fields and a keyboard with the @ sign for email fields make a big difference on mobile.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Show errors instantly and politely:</b> An error message that clears all fields after submission loses the visitor for good.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Use a thank-you page:</b> It tells visitors what happens next and makes conversions easier to measure.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 4.5-3.4 8.3-8 9-4.6-.7-8-4.5-8-9V6l8-3z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg><div><b>KVKK reminder:</b> If you collect personal data through a form, you need to link to your privacy notice and use a separate, unticked checkbox for marketing communications. This note is not legal advice; for details, see our article on <a href="kvkk-uyumlu-web-sitesi">KVKK-compliant websites</a> and consult a legal professional.</div></div>
      <h2 id="guven" class="bi-h2" data-n="06">Trust signals and social proof</h2>
      <p>If visitors don’t know you, they look for proof before deciding. In the same Baymard research, about one in five users said they abandoned a purchase because they didn’t trust the site enough with their card details. Trust is not decoration; it is a precondition for conversion.</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Real references:</b> Client logos, testimonials backed by names and photos, examples of completed projects.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Concrete numbers:</b> How many years have you been in business, how many projects have you completed? Write the ones that can be verified.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Transparent contact details:</b> A full address, landline, map and working hours.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Technical trust signals:</b> HTTPS, an up-to-date design and pages that work without errors. Even one broken link shakes trust.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Rather than hiding references on a separate page, place them close to the moment of decision: a customer testimonial next to pricing or the quote button is far more effective than a logo at the bottom of the “About us” page.</p>
      <h2 id="surtunme" class="bi-h2" data-n="07">Reduce friction: the small obstacles that wear visitors down</h2>
      <p>What kills conversions is usually not one big mistake but small obstacles piling up. Looking at the abandonment reasons on Baymard’s list makes the picture clear: unexpected extra costs, forced account creation, long and complicated processes, site errors. None of these are beyond the design team’s control.</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A simple menu:</b> Five to seven items in the main menu are enough for most sites. Label menu items with the words customers search for, not internal jargon.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Respectful pop-ups:</b> Newsletter pop-ups that cover the screen as soon as the page opens drive visitors away before they see the content. If needed, show them toward the end of the page and make them easy to close.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Transparent prices and terms:</b> Show shipping, installation or extra fees from the start, not at the last step. Surprise costs are the most common reason for abandonment.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Don’t force account creation:</b> Offer guest checkout in e-commerce; suggest creating an account after the purchase.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Alternative contact options:</b> Keep phone and WhatsApp options visible for visitors who don’t want to fill in a form.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Go through a full transaction on your own site as a customer: choose a product, fill in a form, try it on your phone. Every point that makes you hesitate even for a second is where you are losing some of your visitors.</p>
      <h2 id="hiz-mobil" class="bi-h2" data-n="08">Speed and mobile experience: the invisible conversion killer</h2>
      <p>A slow-loading page doesn’t even give visitors a chance to see your design. Google measures page experience with three metrics called Core Web Vitals and recommends the following thresholds for a good experience on web.dev:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What does it measure?</th><th>Good value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)</td><td>Load time of the main content</td><td>2.5 seconds or less</td></tr>
          <tr><td>INP (Interaction to Next Paint)</td><td>Responsiveness to clicks</td><td>200 milliseconds or less</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)</td><td>Elements shifting while the page loads</td><td>0.1 or less</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>These aren’t just SEO metrics; they directly affect conversion. A page that shifts just as you’re about to tap a button, or a form that doesn’t respond, is enough to make a visitor give up. We cover the details of speed optimisation in our article on <a href="site-hizi-core-web-vitals">site speed and Core Web Vitals</a>.</p>
      <p>On mobile, the phone number should also be callable with a single tap, buttons should be large enough to press comfortably with a finger, and pop-ups shouldn’t cover the screen.</p>
      <h2 id="olcum-test" class="bi-h2" data-n="09">Measurement and A/B testing: data, not guesswork</h2>
      <p>Conversion-focused design is not a one-off job but a continuous cycle. Instead of “I think this colour is better” debates, let the data speak.</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 1 — Define your goals:</b> In Google Analytics 4, mark form submissions, phone clicks and WhatsApp clicks as key events.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 2 — Find the leaks:</b> See which pages visitors exit from most and at which form field they give up.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 3 — Watch behaviour:</b> Heatmaps and session recordings show where visitors click and where they get stuck.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 4 — Test one variable:</b> Compare the headline, button copy or form length with an A/B test. If you change everything at once, you won’t know what worked.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 5 — Wait for enough data:</b> On low-traffic sites, a few days of results are misleading; decide based on a meaningful sample.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can find a specific application of these principles for pages receiving ad traffic in <a href="landing-page-rehberi">our landing page guide</a>.</p>
      <div class="bi-check">
        <div class="eyebrow">// QUICK CONVERSION CHECKLIST</div>
        <ul class="bi-list"><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does every page have a single main goal?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the first screen say what you do and who you serve?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the primary button copy describe the outcome?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the form contain only truly necessary fields?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are references and testimonials close to the moment of decision?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are LCP, INP and CLS values in the good range?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are form submissions and phone clicks tracked in analytics?</span></li></ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="sss" class="bi-h2" data-n="10">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bi-faq"><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What is conversion-focused web design?</summary><p>It is a design approach that aims to make it easier for visitors to complete a specific action on the site, such as requesting a quote, buying or calling. Page layout, copy, buttons, forms and speed are built around this goal, and results are measured with data and continuously improved.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What is a good conversion rate?</summary><p>There is no universal right value. Rates vary widely by industry, product price, traffic source and target action. The healthiest comparison is your own site’s performance in previous periods.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>How can I increase my website’s conversion rate?</summary><p>Start by tracking your goals in analytics. Then clarify the first screen, use a single clear call to action, shorten forms, make trust signals visible and improve page speed. Test changes one at a time to see which one works.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What is the relationship between UX and conversion rate?</summary><p>User experience determines how easily visitors find what they are looking for and how effortlessly they complete an action. Confusing menus, long forms and slow pages create friction; as friction decreases, conversion usually increases.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Is A/B testing meaningful for small sites?</summary><p>On low-traffic sites it takes a long time to get statistically significant results. In that case it is more efficient to first implement proven best practices and find problems through user interviews and session recordings; you can introduce A/B testing as traffic grows.</p></details></div>
      <h2 id="sonuc">Final word</h2>
      <p>Conversion-focused web design starts with a clear first screen, calls to action that point to a single goal, short forms, visible trust signals and fast pages, and matures through measurement and testing. The difference between a beautiful site and one that brings in business often lies in these small but deliberate decisions.</p>
      <p>If you would like to examine together where your current site is losing visitors, or build your new site with conversion in mind from the start, <a href="iletisim">write to us</a>. Details of our design and development services are on our <a href="web-cozumleri">web solutions</a> page.</p>
